    {
      "dom_id": "question-83861",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The growth rate of the crab population $P(t)$ in a certain lake is jointly proportional to the crab population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the lake's carrying capacity and the crab population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.05$ and the maximum possible crab population is $1\\,000,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the crab population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the crab population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the crab population, the crab population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.05,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1\\,000.$ Therefore, the crab population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.05P(1\\,000-P).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
